Q: How to place dots when looping through an array in JavaScript A: <p>mmm, you have many errors in your code, first you used localStorage in a wrong way and you miss some brackets, ok let's explain all your mistakes first:</p>
<p><strong>1. Syntax Errors &amp; Invalid JavaScript in <code>load()</code></strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p>The <code>load()</code> function lacks proper opening and closing curly braces (<code>{}</code>).</p>
</li>
<li><p>The expressions inside the <code>for</code> loop (<code>pageY + 'px'</code>, <code>width : '10px'</code>, etc.) are isolated CSS-like properties that are not valid JavaScript code.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>2. Invalid <code>localStorage.getItem()</code> Usage</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><code>localStorage.getItem()</code> takes a single argument (the key name). Passing <code>JSON.stringify(points)</code> as a second argument does not parse or retrieve stored data correctly.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>3. Pushing DOM Elements into an Array for JSON Serialization</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p>The code pushes jQuery/DOM objects (<code>fishElement</code>) directly into the <code>points</code> array (<code>points.push([..., fishElement])</code>).</p>
</li>
<li><p>DOM nodes contain complex circular references and cannot be serialized using <code>JSON.stringify()</code>. Only raw data (like coordinates and text values) should be stored.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>4. Array Initialization Issues</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Initializing <code>var points = [&quot;&quot;]</code> and setting <code>points.length = 20</code> creates an array filled with empty values, leading to errors when looping through it.</li>
</ul>
<p>corrected code:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;style&gt;
  #map { 
    width: 100%; 
    height: 1000px; 
    margin-top: 50px; 
    background: #f0f0f0; 
    position: relative; 
  }
  .dot { 
    position: absolute; 
    width: 10px; 
    height: 10px; 
    background: #000; 
    border-radius: 50%; 
  }
  .fish-label { 
    position: absolute; 
    margin: 0; 
  }
&lt;/style&gt;

&lt;script src=&quot;https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/2.1.1/jquery.min.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;button onclick=&quot;load()&quot;&gt;LOAD&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;button onclick=&quot;save()&quot;&gt;SAVE&lt;/button&gt;

&lt;select id=&quot;myDropdown&quot;&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;shark&quot;&gt;Shark&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;kapador&quot;&gt;Kapador&lt;/option&gt;
&lt;/select&gt;

&lt;div id=&quot;map&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;script&gt;
  var points = [];

  function drawPoint(x, y, fishText) {
    $('#map').append(&quot;&lt;p class='fish-label' style='top: &quot; + y + &quot;px; left: &quot; + x + &quot;px;'&gt;&quot; + fishText + &quot;&lt;/p&gt;&quot;);
    $('#map').append(&quot;&lt;div class='dot' style='top: &quot; + y + &quot;px; left: &quot; + x + &quot;px;'&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&quot;);
  }

  $('#map').click(function (ev) {
    var myFish = $('#myDropdown option:selected').text();
    var x = ev.pageX;
    var y = ev.pageY;

    drawPoint(x, y, myFish);
    points.push({ x: x, y: y, fish: myFish });
  });

  function save() {
    localStorage.setItem('points', JSON.stringify(points));
    alert('Saved successfully!');
  }

  function load() {
    var storedPoints = localStorage.getItem('points');
    if (storedPoints) {
      points = JSON.parse(storedPoints);
      $('#map').empty(); // Clear existing elements before redrawing
      for (var i = 0; i &lt; points.length; i++) {
        drawPoint(points[i].x, points[i].y, points[i].fish);
      }
    }
  }
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Disable selecting some items in a WPF listbox A: <p>Assume that the data class is called <code>Desktop</code> and it has a <code>bool IsValid</code> property.</p>
<p>To disable selection and grey out the text, you can apply this style:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;ListBox.ItemContainerStyle&gt;
    &lt;Style TargetType=&quot;ListBoxItem&quot;&gt;
        &lt;Style.Triggers&gt;
            &lt;DataTrigger Binding=&quot;{Binding IsValid}&quot; Value=&quot;False&quot;&gt;
                &lt;Setter Property=&quot;Foreground&quot; Value=&quot;Gray&quot;/&gt;
                &lt;!-- Both of them can disable click --&gt;
                &lt;Setter Property=&quot;IsHitTestVisible&quot; Value=&quot;False&quot;/&gt;
                &lt;!--Setter Property=&quot;Focusable&quot; Value=&quot;False&quot;/--&gt;
            &lt;/DataTrigger&gt;
        &lt;/Style.Triggers&gt;
    &lt;/Style&gt;
&lt;/ListBox.ItemContainerStyle&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>But for extended selection mode, you need to use the <code>SelectionChanged</code> event to unselect invalid items:</p>
<pre><code>private void OnListBoxSelectionChanged(object sender, SelectionChangedEventArgs e)
{
    if (sender is ListBox listBox)
    {
        var icg = listBox.ItemContainerGenerator;
        foreach (var desktop in e.AddedItems.Cast&lt;Desktop&gt;())
            if (!desktop.IsValid &amp;&amp;
                icg.ContainerFromItem(desktop) is ListBoxItem item)
                item.IsSelected = false;
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Why is &quot;Unidentified&quot; returned on keyboard input on mobile? A: <p>What you need to do is catch the <code>e.target.value</code>:</p>
<pre><code>$(document).on(&quot;keydown&quot;, function(e) {
  var inputValue = e.target.value;
  var lastLetter = inputValue.charAt(inputValue.length - 1);
  alert('Letter: ' + lastLetter);
});
</code></pre>
